course: dictionary
lesson: 6308
title: Small bites and small amounts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A morsel is a small piece or bite of food. When you meet this word in a story, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «Squashed chin-to-foot, she just manages to swallow a morsel (a small bite) of the left-hand bit.» The brackets give the meaning, and «just manages» tells you the bite is tiny.
explain: Morsel can also mean a small amount of anything, not only food. «Not a morsel of sense» means there was no sense at all, not even a tiny bit. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Older books use words like morsel more often. In The Wind in the Willows the animals share every morsel at their feasts, so you can guess that a morsel is a small piece of food that is shared out. Morsel is a noun, so you can say a morsel or morsels.
fact: Morsel is a noun: a morsel, morsels.
fact: «Morsel» means a small piece or bite of food.
fact: «Morsel» can also mean a small amount of anything, as in «not a morsel of sense».
fact: Hungry characters in stories often search for a morsel to eat.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word bit and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Squashed chin-to-foot, she just manages to swallow a morsel of the left-hand bit.» What is a morsel here? | A glass of water | *A small bite of food | A large meal | A long walk
exercise: «There was not a morsel of sense in what he said.» What does morsel mean here? | A loud noise | A large piece of meat | *A tiny amount, none at all | A kind of sweet
exercise: The animals share every morsel at their feasts. What does morsel mean? | A type of animal | *A small piece of food | A big party | A cooking pot
test: Hungry characters search for a morsel to eat. What is a morsel? | A house | *A small piece of food | A song | A river
test: What does «not a morsel of sense» mean? | A sensible plan | A great deal of sense | *None at all | Some sense, but very little
exam: Morsel is a noun. Which is its plural form? | morsel | morsel's | morseling | *morsels
